  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 234 AM EDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nFor early next week, the potent low pressure in the northeast US \nwill shift more northwards as a mid-to-upper level shortwave \ntrough pushes across the central US and deepens across the Mid-\nAtlantic and Southeast US regions. As this trough swings through \nthe Florida Peninsula in the Monday to Tuesday time frame, low \nlevel flow will shift back to a southerly to southwesterly \ndirection and result in the return of deeper tropical moisture \nadvection. With this increasing moisture returning and the \nshortwave trough providing some upper level forcing for ascent, \nrain chances will increase back to the 60-80% range by Tuesday and\ninto the middle of next week across South Florida. Steady \nmoisture advection is then expected to continue through the end of\nthe week.\n\nFor the end of the week, ensembles are hinting that the subtropical \nhigh in the western Atlantic will re-build and expand further east, \nresulting in a return to steady easterly flow. This setup favors \nthe highest rain chances over SW Florida (65-80%) with slightly \nlower chances across SE Florida (50-60%) by Friday and into next \nweekend. Essentially, this pattern suggests the most widespread \nactivity will be seen over SW Florida with more scattered activity\nover the east coast metro areas.\n\nHigh temperatures will rebound slightly through the period, with \nhighs each day reaching anywhere from the upper 80s to low 90s.\nOvernight lows will be a few degrees cooler through Monday night \nwith the front clearing the area, with lows near the lake region in \nthe low to mid 60s and the rest of the region seeing lows in the \nupper 60s to low 70s. By Tuesday night and beyond, overnight lows \nwill increase back into the mid to upper 70s.",
